 little different what's wrong  with capitalism  well capitalism is not a perfect system  but i think  one has to bear in mind first of all  that we in the west don't think of  social systems as utopias  the way marxists conceived of communism  as a utopia as a perfection  we realize the imperfection of our  institutions and arrangements though we  strive gradually if we can to improve  them  capitalism has many things wrong with it  if it's unchecked it can be very various  it can be very insensitive to human  values  it can be cruel to the weak  it in a sense in its purest sense  enshrines darwinism to survival of the  fittest but that's why over the years we  have tried to combine capitalism  with increasing measures of social  responsibility  social sensitivity  attempts to  indirectly  recreate even opportunities for every  generation  but without stifling  individual initiative and self-interest  which are very deeply felt instincts  that if properly channeled  can make a society creative and wealthy  and if suppressed produce the kind of  stagnation which i call the grand  failure  how do you write books  what's your what's your pattern  well  that's i'm using actually this is not my  first book i've written a number of  books before  and until this one i used to write by  hand  i would outline it first of all very  systematically i always have a concept  of a book in my mind and in fact  that kind of  germinates within me gradually very  often for example on sundays i sit in  church  ideas will begin to form in my mind and  my mind sort of operates on its own and  then i begin to feel after some months  that there's something i want to say and  i start outlining it and i never start  writing a book until i have a full  outline ready and then until this book i  used to write it by hand then revise it  and then dictate it into a dictating  machine have it transcribed and then  edit it and so forth  this one i received out of the blue from  the apple  corporation  a macintosh computer and a printer as a  gift and from a chief from a senior  executive there  who thought  that i might be interested in because i  once visited their headquarters  i had never thought of buying a computer  in a word processor  and i started playing around with it  and i started enjoying it  and then at that particular moment i had  some ideas along these lines but i  really wasn't thinking writing a book  but since i had this damn  contraption in front of me that i  enjoyed playing with i said well i'm  going to start outlining on this thing  and see how it works and i started  outlining it  on the word processor on the screen  and then printed it out and i said well  gee this looks like a little outline why  don't i start flashing it out a little  bit and lo and behold just during the  summer month i wrote the first chapter  which is called the grand failure which  gives the title of the book  and then i said to myself well  obviously i have a book here so i'll  continue writing it and  that's how it came into being i noticed  that in the author's notes it says big  new brzezinski northeast harbor maine  august 31 1988. did you write this in  maine that's exactly right that's where  i spend my summer holidays at least  august nothing past summer how long does  it take you to write a book like this  well this one took me about  well from the writing process took me  about 15 months  or so maybe a little less than that the  actual the actual writing process i  started writing it probably  a year earlier maybe in july june  somewhere around there  you have to worry about the fact that  once it's written it takes some months  to get it into print yeah and that  things can change  yes but  for example in this particular book i  say in the preface that this book was  completed in august of 88 and there will  be events taking place  which will outpace the book but that i  hope the reader will find that the book  gives them a framework for understanding  what is happening that is helpful to him  and i really do believe quite seriously  although obviously i'm biased since it's  my book but i believe if someone reads  this book and then reads the newspapers  about let's say national riots and the  soviet union  or the new maritime economic zones in  china  or the discussions in hungary in poland  about pluralism  and changes of the existing political  system that this book will give them a  framework  for understanding the significance of  these developments and a framework which  makes them alert to the fact  that we're living really through  momentous historical change  that this pertains to the fact that an  ideology which shaped much of the  century  which was the dominant force that  influenced our lives for better for  worse  before our very eyes is disintegrating  and dying now this is a very major  historical development that we are  entering the post-communist era  an era in which communism is no longer  going to be a major philosophical  political force in world affairs this is  a threshold  era in that sense and i hope that's what  the reader will get out of it and then  when he reads a newspaper watches  television about yerevan and the riots  in armenia or about the lithuanians  demanding freedom etc he'll have a  framework in which he can understand  these events you asked me earlier about  american news reporting  it's very good when it comes to  reporting events  it's not so good  when it comes to interpreting them  seeing them as part of larger historical  trends  and i think my book perhaps  helps the reader  to understand what is actually happening  and not just to what is in the book  what else happens when you write a book  who will for sure read a book like this  let's go to the present united states  the president of the united states has  already been reading it i would have a  note from him  and  we had suffered not long ago and we  talked about it  so when you sat down to write it did you  think that that would eventually happen  i mean is that always going in your mind  are you looking at it in your own mind's  eye an audience out there of power  well  yeah but not necessarily of the  president although  in this particular case  i was aware of the fact the president  and i know each other and that i would  certainly send him a copy i didn't know  whether he would read it or not  and but that's happened i wrote another  book  prior to this one called game plan which  was more of a strategic book  which dealt with weapons and geopolitics  and i sent a copy of it to reagan on i  remember thursday morning thursday  afternoon he phoned me up  and told me i've just read the following  20 pages in it and told me what pages he  read so i immediately went to the book  and looked up what he had read and was a  section on the sdi the strategic defense  initiative  but when i write a book like this i  really write it for a larger audience i  would hope that people who  realize that our lives and the lives of  our children are influenced by what  happens in the world  and have some interest there for what is  happening in the world  either because they read  major newspapers or they read magazines  like foreign affairs or they watch  the tv shows that pertain to world  affairs  will find the book helpful  in understanding and the real important  word here is understanding what is  happening in world affairs it isn't what  the facts what facts they get out of  this book because i can find those facts  elsewhere probably  it's not that the book contains  sensational new facts it's the  interpretation the understanding that  they gain  so that when they think about the world  they'll have a new insight into what is  happening in the world for example a  very simple one among others communism  is dying  a very major change is taking place what  is its significance what does it portend  for eastern europe for china for the  soviet union for world affairs  what does it  pretend for us  i think it pretends for us that the  soviet challenge henceforth would be  only military  is the soviet military any good yes it's  pretty good it's not  as good as they have thought and all  military discovers shortcomings where it  gets involved in combat after prolonged  period of peace so we discovered that in  afghanistan but there's no doubt that  soviet equipment is good that's the one  area of soviet industry where they  produce good stuff why are they able to  produce good military equipment and  nothing else  because they have a motivation  whereas nowhere else do they have a  motivation they don't have a consumer  market they don't have competition  but in this area they do have  competition  and they concentrate their resources  any reaction from the president after he  read the book what did he tell you about  well he told me he was reading it and  you know that's good enough for me and  because i saw him couple days afterwards  no i have not talked to him since when  you see a president and you're no longer  in government at the moment  what goes on in your own mind about what  kind of things you want him  to know  during a meeting like that well  it's probably the same thing that i  always had in my mind when i dealt with  the president i was actually working for  namely  i've always been very conscious of the  fact that the president is saturated  with information  torn as we talked earlier by conflicting  pools  and that therefore i render him no  service  by coming in with another two-bit idea  or another bit of some fact  factology as i call it  what the president needs very much and  very rarely gets  is some  significant generalization  or some integrative proposal that  enables him  to  move forward if you will  strategically yeah so when i speak to a  president unless i'm engaging in social  chit chat  i try to  give him if i can  some sort of broad formulation that  helps him  grasp  various  parts of a picture  and see it in a larger frame  either as an initiative that you have to  undertake or as an insight that you got  to have  because i think that is where presidents  are  worst served by the system and even by  their advisors  are there a couple of camps that are  operating now in this town when it comes  to foreign policy  no not yet not yet  i think for one thing the administration  has  a not fully organized itself  in foreign affairs the only shop that's  really in good shape as of our  conversation today  is brent scowcroft's national security  council the state department hasn't yet  been put together and of course the  unfortunate delays connected with the  town nomination  have prevented the defense department  from being put together  so that the president's for in the  president's national security team  not at the very top but at the echelons  below it  has not been fully put together  now until that has happened and until  they will have had time to meet and  discuss some foreign policy issues will  not have these camps the camps will  surface once there are divisions and  real also conflicts of personality  that's too early for that  this may not be possible but what would  be the two different camps on an issue  like the soviet union right now  well i think the two camps would be very  simply put those who would say let's be  prudent let's wait let's not do much  and those who would say  gorbachev needs our help let's rush in  give him help those are very extreme and  broad over simplification where are you  i would lean towards the first  what about china  well on china i don't think there's too  much division i think everybody  recognizes that the american chinese  relationship has evolved very well  first president nixon  broke through and established a contact  then president carter broke through and  normalized relations  since then the political the economic  the military relationship has grown well  and president bush has a lot of feeding  and sensitivity for china he understands  china well  he is in my judgment  the first president we have had  for whom our relationship with the  pacific  is just spontaneously naturally felt to  be as important as our relationship with  the atlantic japan  well the same thing goes for japan he  has that feeling and that grasp and  there's no other relationship with japan  on balance is a good one we have a  number of conflictual issues with the  japanese  but the white house was very right in  saying when bush was leaving for japan  that this today is for us  the single most important bilateral  relationship we have with any country in  the world  and it is true would be the two camps on  japan well the two camps in japan would  be those who would say let's throw the  book at them  let's force them to back down force them  maybe also to harm more and those who  would say  those are important issues but let's put  them in a framework of closer  cooperation so that we become bound more  together and in that context we can work  out these conflictual issues where are  you on the second  central america nicaragua  well  i don't know again whether camps exists  but once they will form it'll probably  be on the question of how how hard we  press  in sustaining the contras  in dealing with panama  and  although you know you can't put these  things together  here i think there's a general problem  that we don't at this stage have policy  i don't think they're camps yet  we don't have a policy what would you do  if you were in the position of making a  decision on what to do with the contra  aid  well  i would certainly  feel that we have a moral obligation  towards the contras we have undertaken  to armed and to send them the battle  then we undercut him i don't think great  power can act like that towards people  we can't yank him around on a yo-yo so i  think the first obligation we have is to  determine what is our long-term strategy  obtain congressional support for it and  then sustain it  but not try to do it from  one battle with congress to another  battle of congress because then we'll  lose some we'll win some but the result  is going to be a very incoherent policy  and i don't think we have sat down and  really decided what our strategy is  once we do i think the thing to do would  be to  truly sit down with chairman pal  chairman nunn  the minority leaders and see if we can  fashion a foreign policy we can sustain  for a few years  this yanking around that we have been  subject to is terribly destructive for  everybody  we've got six or seven minutes left i  want to ask you about students do you  teach kids anymore yeah when i teach  colombia and their kids and some of them  are graduate students some of them are  undergraduates  what do you think of today's student  they're very good very serious very  motivated  there's some interesting changes in them  when i taught earlier at harvard and  columbia more often than not my best  students were jewish kids jewish  americans most often  and today some of my best students are  oriental americans  that's quite an interesting change  korean americans japanese americans  chinese americans  and that i think probably foreshadows  some interesting cultural changes in  american society  but kids that i deal with are very  serious very motivated interested in  policy i don't teach abstract political  science with all the  what i call  theoretical conceptual baloney  i try to teach kids to deal with  politics in a real sense  i have a very large class for example  one of the largest classes in columbia  250 students which i teach as if i was  running a national security council  meeting and i alternatively briefed the  president in it  then i argue  as if i was in the secretary of state  then i give the defense secretary's  point of view then i inject some  warnings from the cia then the domestic  advisors of the president tell the  president of the domestic dilemmas  then i again as national security  adviser outlined the options for the  president three or four options  that he has to pick and give the pros  and cons and i try to balance the  advantages and the disadvantages then i  ask the class to vote on the options and  to get them engaged in which ones they  prefer and then bang i become the  president announce my choice and dismiss  the class the students love it  and then i teach a seminar in which for  example the last one i taught it as if  we were a bunch of advisors to the  president-elect  giving him advice on the foreign policy  he should pursue the next four years  students had to prepare papers on that  but the one who's presenting it  would always represent the paper on the  subject regarding which he also hoped to  become the apunti  so he would want to be the secretary of  state or the fans or maybe an assistant  secretary for europe  and then the other members of the  president's team all of whom share the  desire that i'm elected  would critique him  in such a manner as to expose the  weaknesses and in the hope that one of  them  would replace that person as the nominee  for that job  and i taught the students first of all  that you have to demonstrate that you  know the subject better but then  secondly don't be too aggressive  if you're overly aggressive you're not a  team player you won't get that [ __ ]  either  so i try to teach them what real  politics is about and not a lot of  theoretical  paradigms and concepts which prepare you  in life to do on the one thing  to teach other people the same concepts  and patterns so that they in turn can  teach them other people i usually begin  my class by telling my students look in  recent history in america there have  only been two people who study political  science have played a major role in  washington kissinger 9.  the rest have been played by lawyers and  economists historians  that's because political science has  been taught in a very sterile fashion  which doesn't prepare you  for dealing with the life of real  politics  students seem to like those lessons  what's what are the what's the politics  of a student today compared to what they  may have been 20 years ago very  conservative  much more conservative  i organize my students sometimes in  teams  you know and i deliberately try to  structure them sort of soft line  hardline etc and then have clashes and  lately i've had many more volunteers for  the hard  approach over the south approach  what's causing that well cultural change  in the country the country's more  conservative you see that in the  election of reagan  and what's causing that  well that was of course much more  complicated i suppose vietnam has a lot  to do with it  a general sense of drift that developed  in the course of the 70s  requests for some deeper verities  but i think the picture culture is very  mixed because we are simultaneously  maybe a more conservative society  but we're also a more hedonistic society  with focus on self-gratification  self-fulfillment  those are troubling signs  where is that going to lead us  well as i said these are troubling signs  because that could mean  cultural dissolution  loss of  the kind of values that make a society  great competitive assertive  certain degree of self-righteousness  and commitment  are necessary ingredient ingredients  of social cohesion and social dynamism  but if those troubling signs came to  life would that or or they followed to a  maturity would that mean that capitalism  then was its own victim  well it could but that i doubt because  you know the one area where capitalism  is really successful is in gratifying  material wants  and the desire for  the satisfaction of material ones is  still a very major source  of social motivation  so i don't think it's going to be a  crisis of capitalism as such because of  these cultural difficulties it could be  simply a crisis of  social commitment social virility  we could become a decadent society  that's not the crisis of capitalism it's  a crisis however our culture and if we  become a decadent society then we could  be vulnerable  this is the book it's called the grand  failure and our special guest is right  there on the covers big new brzezinski  who's former national security adviser  to president jimmy carter is currently a  counselor with the center for strategic  and international studies and also a  professor at columbia university in new  york  thank you very much for joining us this  is big new brzezinski i enjoy it thank  you  [Music]  this interview with sebignor brzezinski  will re-air monday morning at about 6 20  a.m eastern time 3 20 pacific  and join us next week on book notes for  an interview with bruce odes the author  of from the president richard nixon's  secret files that's coming up next  sunday at 8pm eastern time  stay with us now as capital agenda looks  at what's ahead on capitol hill that's  coming up next  [Music]  up next capital agenda c-span's weekly  look at hearings legislation and forms  scheduled

